# Proof that $g$ is of bounded variation on $[0,1]$ for $\beta > 1$

## Setup

Let $g(x) = x^\beta \cos(1/x)$ for $x \in (0,1]$ and $g(0)=0$, with $\beta > 1$.

**Continuity at $0$.** Since $|\cos(1/x)|\le 1$ and $\beta>0$, we have $|g(x)|\le x^\beta \to 0 = g(0)$ as $x\to 0^+$. So $g$ is continuous on $[0,1]$.

## The derivative on $(0,1)$

For $x\in(0,1)$,
$$g'(x) = \beta x^{\beta-1}\cos(1/x) + x^\beta\cdot\sin(1/x)\cdot\frac{1}{x^2} = \beta x^{\beta-1}\cos(1/x) + x^{\beta-2}\sin(1/x).$$

## Integrability of $|g'|$

We show $g'\in L^1([0,1])$ when $\beta>1$.

**Term 1:** $\displaystyle\int_0^1 \bigl|\beta x^{\beta-1}\cos(1/x)\bigr|\,dx \le \beta\int_0^1 x^{\beta-1}\,dx = \beta\cdot\frac{1}{\beta}=1$, since $\beta-1>-1$.

**Term 2:** $\displaystyle\int_0^1 x^{\beta-2}|\sin(1/x)|\,dx$. Substitute $u=1/x$, so $x=1/u$, $dx = -u^{-2}\,du$:
$$\int_0^1 x^{\beta-2}|\sin(1/x)|\,dx = \int_1^\infty u^{-(\beta-2)}\,|\sin u|\,u^{-2}\,du = \int_1^\infty \frac{|\sin u|}{u^\beta}\,du.$$

This converges if and only if $\beta > 1$, since $|\sin u|$ has positive mean and $\int_1^\infty u^{-\beta}\,du$ converges exactly when $\beta>1$. (Concretely: on each interval $[n\pi+\pi/6,\, n\pi+5\pi/6]$, $|\sin u|\ge 1/2$, so the integral dominates $\frac12\sum_n \int_{n\pi+\pi/6}^{n\pi+5\pi/6}u^{-\beta}\,du \asymp \sum_n n^{-\beta}$, which converges iff $\beta>1$; the upper bound $\int_1^\infty u^{-\beta}\,du$ also converges iff $\beta>1$.)

Since $\beta>1$ by hypothesis, $g'\in L^1([0,1])$.

## Absolute continuity (hence bounded variation)

For any $\varepsilon>0$, $g$ is $C^1$ on $[\varepsilon,1]$, so
$$g(x) - g(\varepsilon) = \int_\varepsilon^x g'(t)\,dt \qquad (x\in[\varepsilon,1]).$$

Let $\varepsilon\to 0^+$: $g(\varepsilon)\to 0 = g(0)$ by continuity, and $\int_\varepsilon^x g'\to \int_0^x g'$ since $g'\in L^1$. Therefore
$$g(x) = g(0) + \int_0^x g'(t)\,dt \qquad \text{for all } x\in[0,1].$$

This is exactly the defining property of absolute continuity. Every absolutely continuous function is of bounded variation, with
$$V_0^1(g) \le \int_0^1 |g'(t)|\,dt < \infty.$$

## Conclusion

For every $\beta > 1$, the function $g(x)=x^\beta\cos(1/x)$ (with $g(0)=0$) is absolutely continuous on $[0,1]$, and therefore of bounded variation on $[0,1]$.

